import{_ as e,o as a,c as i,an as r}from"./chunks/framework.DwXkzB5Y.js";const o="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/assets/wumrdwv.T-y7wHBp.png",f=JSON.parse('{"title":"States, thresholds and traits","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"manual/states_traits.md","filePath":"manual/states_traits.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"manual/states_traits.md"};function l(h,t,n,d,c,p){return a(),i("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[r(`<h1 id="States,-thresholds-and-traits" tabindex="-1">States, thresholds and traits <a class="header-anchor" href="#States,-thresholds-and-traits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;States, thresholds and traits {#States,-thresholds-and-traits}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The documentation of HeatExchange.jl sets out why the things it computes <a href="https://biophysicalecology.github.io/HeatExchange.jl/dev/manual/units_traits#State-variables-are-not-traits" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">are not traits</a>. Body temperature is a <em>state variable</em>. Metabolic rate and water loss are <em>processes</em>. Each belongs to an organism in a place at a time. What can be a trait is a particular value of a state or process at which something happens to the organism, or at which it acts: a <em>threshold</em>.</p><p>This package is where thresholds live. A heat budget returns a body temperature of 60 °C without comment. The model of behaviour says that 60 °C will not do, and what the organism does instead.</p><h2 id="Four-kinds-of-term" tabindex="-1">Four kinds of term <a class="header-anchor" href="#Four-kinds-of-term" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Four kinds of term {#Four-kinds-of-term}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A model of an organism as a dynamical system has four kinds of term (Kearney et al. 2021). Behaviour adds to each.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Term</th><th style="text-align:left;">In HeatExchange.jl</th><th style="text-align:left;">Added here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>state variable</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">core, skin and surface temperatures</td><td style="text-align:left;">where the organism is: shade, height, depth; what it is doing: <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.Resting"><code>Resting</code></a>, <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.Basking"><code>Basking</code></a>, <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.Active"><code>Active</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>process</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">heat flows, metabolic rate, water loss</td><td style="text-align:left;">none: the processes of the heat budget, evaluated in the state behaviour has chosen</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>environmental variable</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">air, ground and sky temperatures, wind, humidity, radiation</td><td style="text-align:left;">the set of environments within reach, see <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.AvailableEnvironments"><code>AvailableEnvironments</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>parameter</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the properties of the organism</td><td style="text-align:left;">the range over which each can be changed, and the thresholds at which it is</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Threshold-traits-of-an-ectotherm" tabindex="-1">Threshold traits of an ectotherm <a class="header-anchor" href="#Threshold-traits-of-an-ectotherm" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Threshold traits of an ectotherm {#Threshold-traits-of-an-ectotherm}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For an ectotherm the thresholds are body temperatures, fields of <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.EctothermBehavioralLimits"><code>EctothermBehavioralLimits</code></a>:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">limits </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> example_ectotherm_behavioral_limits</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="vp-raw-html"><div class="markdown"><table><tr><th align="left">Threshold</th><th align="left">Value</th><th align="left">What happens there</th></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>critical_temperature_min</code></td><td align="left">6.0 °C</td><td align="left">below it the animal cannot move; it retreats if it can</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>emerge_temperature_min</code></td><td align="left">15.0 °C</td><td align="left">the body temperature at which it can leave its retreat</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>basking_temperature_min</code></td><td align="left">17.5 °C</td><td align="left">above it the animal may be out basking, but not yet foraging</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>active_temperature_min</code></td><td align="left">24.0 °C</td><td align="left">the lower bound of the range for activity</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>target_temperature</code></td><td align="left">30.0 °C</td><td align="left">the preferred temperature: above it, the animal begins to respond to heat</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>active_temperature_max</code></td><td align="left">34.0 °C</td><td align="left">the upper bound of the range for activity</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>critical_temperature_max</code></td><td align="left">40.0 °C</td><td align="left">above it the animal cannot move; used when choosing a depth to retreat to</td></tr></table>
</div></div><p>These are the <code>CT_min</code>, <code>T_RB_min</code>, <code>T_B_min</code>, <code>T_F_min</code>, <code>T_pref</code>, <code>T_F_max</code> and <code>CT_max</code> of the NicheMapR ectotherm model (Kearney and Porter 2020). They are properties of the animal: measured in a thermal gradient or observed in the field, independent of the weather, with dimensions of temperature.</p><p>Together they divide the state variable into bands, and the band decides the consequence: <img src="`+o+`" alt="" width="720px" height="170px"></p><p>An hour in which the animal can be active is an hour in which it can feed. The count of such hours is a main output of a mechanistic niche model, and it comes from two thresholds applied to a computed state.</p><h2 id="Threshold-traits-of-an-endotherm" tabindex="-1">Threshold traits of an endotherm <a class="header-anchor" href="#Threshold-traits-of-an-endotherm" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Threshold traits of an endotherm {#Threshold-traits-of-an-endotherm}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For an endotherm the thresholds are mostly limits on processes and parameters, fields of <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.ThermoregulationLimits"><code>ThermoregulationLimits</code></a>:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">limits </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> example_thermoregulation_limits</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">()</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="vp-raw-html"><div class="markdown"><table><tr><th align="left">Threshold</th><th align="left">Value</th><th align="left">Role</th></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>minimum_heat_flow</code></td><td align="left">77.62 W</td><td align="left">the least heat the animal can produce: the reference of the controller</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>core_temperature.reference</code></td><td align="left">37.0 °C</td><td align="left">the core temperature that is defended</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>core_temperature.max</code></td><td align="left">39.0 °C</td><td align="left">the highest core temperature tolerated</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>flesh_conductivity.max</code></td><td align="left">2.8 W K^-1 m^-1</td><td align="left">flesh conductivity at full vasodilation</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>axis_ratio_factor.max</code></td><td align="left">5.0</td><td align="left">the most elongated posture</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>panting.pant.max</code></td><td align="left">10.0</td><td align="left">the greatest multiple of resting ventilation</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><code>skin_wetness.max</code></td><td align="left">1.0</td><td align="left">the greatest fraction of the skin that can be wet</td></tr></table>
</div></div><p>The minimum metabolic rate is a threshold on a process: a solution of the heat budget below it is the signal that the animal must act. The others are the ends of the ranges over which a parameter can be changed.</p><h2 id="Plastic-parameters" tabindex="-1">Plastic parameters <a class="header-anchor" href="#Plastic-parameters" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plastic parameters {#Plastic-parameters}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A parameter in HeatExchange.jl has one value. Many are not constant in a living animal: fur is raised and flattened, skin is wetted, blood is sent to the skin. A <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.SteppedParameter"><code>SteppedParameter</code></a> describes a parameter the organism can change:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">limits</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">flesh_conductivity</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;">SteppedParameter{Quantity{Float64, 𝐋 𝐌 𝚯^-1 𝐓^-3, Unitful.FreeUnits{(K^-1, m^-1, W), 𝐋 𝐌 𝚯^-1 𝐓^-3, nothing}}, Quantity{Float64, 𝐋 𝐌 𝚯^-1 𝐓^-3, Unitful.FreeUnits{(K^-1, m^-1, W), 𝐋 𝐌 𝚯^-1 𝐓^-3, nothing}}}(0.9 W K^-1 m^-1, 0.9 W K^-1 m^-1, 2.8 W K^-1 m^-1, 0.1 W K^-1 m^-1)</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Field</th><th style="text-align:left;">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>current</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the value now. A state, changed by the controller</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>reference</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the value in the resting or heat-conserving condition</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>max</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the limit of the response</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>step</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the increment by which a rule-based controller changes it</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>reference</code> and <code>max</code> are traits. They bound the plasticity of the parameter and can be measured: the flesh conductivity of a vasoconstricted and a vasodilated limb, the depth of a flattened and a raised coat. <code>current</code> is a state. <code>step</code> belongs to the numerical method, not the animal, though it should not be finer than the animal&#39;s own control.</p><p>So a parameter of the heat budget becomes, here, a state with trait-valued bounds. In that sense physiological and behavioural thermoregulation are the same thing: both move a parameter of the physics between limits that are properties of the organism.</p><h2 id="What-is-not-a-threshold-trait" tabindex="-1">What is not a threshold trait <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-is-not-a-threshold-trait" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is not a threshold trait {#What-is-not-a-threshold-trait}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p><strong>Critical air temperatures.</strong> The lower and upper critical temperatures of an endotherm, and its thermoneutral zone, are <em>air</em> temperatures. They belong to the environment, and hold only for the wind, humidity, radiation and posture of the measurement (Kearney et al. 2021). Here they are outputs, see <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/tutorials/mammal#The-critical-temperatures-are-outputs">A mammal across air temperatures</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Not to be confused with critical body temperatures.</strong> The critical thermal limits of an ectotherm are <em>body</em> temperatures, and are traits. The difference is whether the value is a property of the state of the organism or of the conditions that produce it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Patterns of behaviour.</strong> Hours of activity, the depth of a burrow used, and the shade selected are states, however often they are reported as traits. This package computes them.</p></li></ul><h2 id="The-four-classes-of-functional-trait" tabindex="-1">The four classes of functional trait <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-four-classes-of-functional-trait" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The four classes of functional trait {#The-four-classes-of-functional-trait}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In the classification of Kearney et al. (2021):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Class</th><th style="text-align:left;">In BiophysicalBehaviour.jl</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>parameter</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the <code>reference</code> and <code>max</code> of each <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.SteppedParameter"><code>SteppedParameter</code></a>; the cost of panting; the weights of the optimisation, which state the relative cost of each response</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>threshold</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the body temperatures of <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.EctothermBehavioralLimits"><code>EctothermBehavioralLimits</code></a>; <code>minimum_heat_flow</code> and the maximum core temperature of <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.ThermoregulationLimits"><code>ThermoregulationLimits</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>model</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">the thermal strategy, <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.Ectotherm"><code>Ectotherm</code></a> or <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.Endotherm"><code>Endotherm</code></a>; the activity period; the capability flags <code>can_climb</code>, <code>can_retreat_underground</code>, <code>can_seek_shade</code>, <code>can_pant</code> and the rest; the <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.AbstractThermoregulationMode"><code>AbstractThermoregulationMode</code></a>; which part of the body holds the lungs</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>estimation</strong></td><td style="text-align:left;">preferred temperatures from a thermal gradient, field body temperatures of active animals, evaporative water loss against air temperature in a metabolic chamber: observations from which thresholds and limits are estimated, given the conditions of measurement</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The model traits are types and flags, on which methods are chosen. The parameter and threshold traits are the data of a limits struct, see <a href="./parameters">Parameters</a>. The states are what <a href="/BiophysicalBehaviour.jl/previews/PR28/api#BiophysicalBehaviour.thermoregulate"><code>thermoregulate</code></a> returns.</p>`,31)])])}const u=e(s,[["render",l]]);export{f as __pageData,u as default};
